<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.14.3: The Connection between Sequences and Terms</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01118</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.14.3: The Connection between Sequences and Terms</md:title>
    <md:uuid>952dc309-bbb5-41dd-82f4-3933113af5a7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.geogebra.org/material/iframe/id/qugvaudj/width/681/height/350/border/888888/sfsb/true/smb/false/stb/false/stbh/false/ai/false/asb/false/sri/true/rc/false/ld/false/sdz/false/ctl/false"/>
      </media>
      <para>Some checkers are lined up, with blue on one side, red on the other, and 1 empty space between them. A <emphasis>move</emphasis> in this checker game pushes any checker forward 1 space, or jumps over any 1 checker of the other color. Jumping the same color is not allowed, moving backward is not allowed, and 2 checkers cannot occupy the same space.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="THREE BLACK CHECKERS, A SPACE, AND THEN THREE RED CHECKERS.&#10;">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_17_06_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>You complete the puzzle by switching the colors completely: ending up with black on the right, red on the left, and 1 empty space between them.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Using 1 checker on each side, complete the puzzle. What is the smallest number of moves needed?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>3 moves</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Using 3 checkers on each side, complete the puzzle. What is the smallest number of moves needed?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>15 moves</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Estimate the number of moves needed if there are 2 or 4 checkers on each side. Then test your guesses.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>8 moves and 24 moves</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Noah says he used the solution for 3 checkers on each side to help him solve the puzzle for 4 checkers. Describe how this might happen.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The moves that don’t involve the fourth checker on each side are the same as before, so it is possible to decide the number of moves by running the first half of the three-checker game, then moving the fourth checkers, and then running the second half.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>How many moves do you think it will take to complete a puzzle with 7 checkers on each side?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>63 moves</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check"><title>Self Check</title>
      <para>The table below shows the number of checkers and the number of moves needed to win a skipping game.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>Number of Checkers</emphasis></entry><entry>
              1
            </entry>
            <entry>
              2
            </entry>
            <entry>
              3
            </entry>
            <entry>
              4
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>Number of Skips Needed</emphasis></entry><entry>
              2
            </entry>
            <entry>
              8
            </entry>
            <entry>
              18
            </entry>
            <entry>
              32
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
      <para>How many skips are needed with 6 checkers on each side?</para></note>
      <section>
        <title>Additional Resources</title>
        
  <section><title>Using a Table to Find Missing Values</title>

    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>Number of Checkers</emphasis></entry><entry>
            1
          </entry>
          <entry>
            2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            4
          </entry>
          <entry>
            7
          </entry>
          <entry>
            8
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>Number of Moves Needed</emphasis></entry><entry>
            3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            8
          </entry>
          <entry>
            15
          </entry>
          <entry>
            24
          </entry>
          <entry>
            63
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ?
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para>What is the pattern that you used in the previous activity to find the number of moves depending on the number of
      checkers?</para>
    <para>Each row is multiplied by 1 more, so:</para>
    <para>\(1\cdot3=3\)</para>
    <para>\(2\cdot4=8\)</para>
    <para>\(3\cdot5=15\)</para>
    <para>\(4\cdot6=24\)</para>
    <para>How many moves are needed with 5 checkers?</para>
    <para>\(5\cdot7=35\) moves</para>
    <para>What about 6 checkers?</para>
    <para>\(6\cdot8=48\) moves</para>
    <note class="try look-at-me"/>
    <title>Try It: Using a Table to Find Missing Values</title>
    <example><exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para>Using the table and the pattern, how many moves are needed with 8 checkers?</para>
      
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>Here is how to solve this problem using a general strategy:</para>
      
      <para>The next column in the table had 7 checkers and then multiplied 7 by 9 to get 63 moves.</para>
      
      <para>Next, the 8 would need to be multiplied by 10, so:</para>
      
      <para>\(8\cdot10=80\)</para>
      
      <para>80 moves are needed.</para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    </example></section>
      </section>
      
  </content>
